> I have an idea and I have a proof. The idea is the volume node to volume node plan. The proof is what confirms the idea at the zone. — Source GPS: 3o1VVsfBmmU | [BEGINNING] | Tom's 13-step framework video — Context: This is Tom's own framing. The idea is the directional hypothesis. The proof is the confirmation that makes the idea actionable. Without proof, the idea is just a guess. — 1.2 The Idea — The idea is always a volume-node-to-volume-node plan: — Where is the market now? (current volume node / price area) — Where is the next volume node? (target / destination) — What is the directional hypothesis? (long or short) — The idea comes from: prior day profiles, weekly profiles, VWAP, market structure, multi-asset correlation, opening range, news/gap context. — The idea alone is NEVER enough to trade. — 1.3 The Proof — Proof is the observable market behavior at the zone that confirms the idea. Tom does not use a single proof type. He layers multiple proofs. — 
> It's just like with other setups. You need to know what you're looking for. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [BEGINNING] | CVD Strategy video — Context: Tom states that delta (and by extension, any proof tool) requires filters and context. Proof without an idea is noise. — 1.4 Proof as Filter, Not Trigger — Tom consistently treats proof as a filter that validates the idea, not as a standalone trigger. The zone comes first (from zone_identification_rules.md). The proof comes second. The execution mode comes third (from setup_type_rules.md). — 
> If we are able to predefine support and resistance, then it's enjoyable to work with these advances. — Source GPS: oa8kxSLLkAo | [END] | Footprint tutorial — Context: Tom explicitly states that footprint (a proof tool) works best when layered on top of predefined S/R levels (zones). — END SECTION 1 — Section 2: Proof Type Taxonomy — This section catalogs all distinct proof types found across Tom's extractions. Each type is tagged with: — Definition: What Tom says it is — How it works: The mechanics Tom describes — Execution mode context: Which setup types use this proof — Source GPS: Every claim traced to transcript — Gaps: What Tom does NOT define — 2.1 Profile-Based Proof — 2.1.1 B Profile = Weakness at Highs — Definition: A B profile (balance profile after trend) indicates that the market has moved to an extreme and created a balance area at the top. This is a structural sign of weakness. — How it works: — 
