> If I now had liquidity above I'll show you that briefly as well. So if I had a lot of liquidity up here right now above this price range which I'm marking here on the top right in this 5minute chart and if the market were to accumulate large volumes there then yes I would definitely be looking for a long position. But why am I not looking for it? because the initial balance is so large meaning the expansion of the trading opening is just very very big and that means here traders had to buy at the market open at that point they don't really care what the price is because these are hedging transactions and they will certainly be corrected later. — Source GPS: k0zm5NYn0IQ | [MIDDLE] | DAX Crash short — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Large initial balance = overextension = short bias. — 2.10 Exhaustion / Extreme Proof — 2.10.1 Trend Ends with Extremes — Definition: Trends typically end with extreme volume/delta readings that indicate exhaustion. — How it works: — 
> A market, when it is trending, usually ends a trend with extremes. You probably know this from volume spikes in the market and so on. And we usually see such volume spikes reflected in the cumulative delta as well, where we see extreme selling down here. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE-END] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Extreme readings at the end of a trend = exhaustion = reversal setup. — 2.10.2 No Extreme = Trend Continues — Definition: If a trending market does not produce extreme readings, the trend is likely to continue. — How it works: — 
> So, what does it mean for us if it doesn't get big? If it doesn't get big, the probability is simply very high that the market will continue to fall. And only when the extreme is reached, can we assume that there is now a possibility for a reversal. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE-END] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: TREND-CONTINUATION. No extreme = no exhaustion = trend continues. — 2.10.3 Extreme Needs Verification — Definition: Even when an extreme is reached, further verification is required before trading a reversal. — How it works: — 
> And even then we have to look into it again. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE-END] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Extreme alone is not enough. Must be combined with zone + additional proof. — 2.10.4 Exponential Volumes Then Consolidation = Setup — Definition: A sharp move with exponential volumes followed by consolidation creates a setup for a reversal or continuation trade. — How it works: — 
> That means nothing other than a market has risen sharply with absolutely exponential volumes, and suddenly there's a period of consolidation within the chart. — Source GPS: tS6ZEiHj8gQ | [BEGINNING] | Perfect Short Trade — 
> Namely, the total volumes are increasing at a steady rate. — Source GPS: tS6ZEiHj8gQ | [BEGINNING] | Perfect Short Trade — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Exponential volume + consolidation = potential reversal setup. — 2.11 CVD / Delta Proof — 2.11.1 Pressure Easing When Delta Shrinks — Definition: When price continues to drop but delta shrinks, selling pressure is easing. — How it works: — 
> That's an indication for me. That shows me the pressure is easing. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Shrinking delta on continued drop = sellers losing strength. — 2.11.2 Neutrality + Drop = Buyer Problem — Definition: When cumulative delta is neutral (no extreme) but price is dropping, the buyers who drove price up are losing control. — How it works: — 
> I see neutrality. So, there is no more extreme value. But the market is going down. What does that tell me? To me, that immediately and clearly says that this buyer here, who drove the market up, has a problem. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: TREND-CONTINUATION breakdown. Neutrality on delta + price drop = buyer weakness = breakdown likely. — 2.11.3 No Support on Cumulative Delta = Range Break Likely — Definition: When a range low is tested and cumulative delta shows no buying support, the range is likely to break. — How it works: — 
> at the moment when I see right here that there is no longer strong support down here, I can see that here on the cumulative delta, it's not being strongly supported, so not being strongly bought. For me, it's very clear that the probability of the range breaking to the downside is high now. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: BREAKOUT. No delta support at range boundary = breakout likely. — 2.11.4 No Extreme Buyers = Sudden Drop — Definition: When there are no extreme buyers present in a range, the probability of a sudden drop is high. — How it works: — 
> I can see there are no extreme buyers present. And so the probability is high that it will just drop suddenly. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: BREAKOUT. Absence of extreme buyers = no support = sudden drop. — 2.11.5 Delta at Technical Levels Only — Definition: Delta extremes must be evaluated at technical levels, not in the middle of nowhere. — How it works: — 
> What is also very, very, very important and crucial is, for example, was there a technical support level here or did this happen somewhere in the middle? Because if I also have a strong technical level here, then I know there was an exhaustion here, which means an extremum. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: Universal. Delta proof is only valid at predefined zones. — 2.12 Volume Profile Proof — 2.12.1 Volume Structure (Empty Space) = Directional Bias — Definition: When volume is clustered at two price areas with empty space between, the probability of a move through the empty space is high. — How it works: — 
> you can already see here there's volume down here and there's volume up there. In between there's a bit of an empty space and as long as we hold these volumes here for me, the probability is there that the market can shoot upwards. — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [MIDDLE] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — Execution mode context: TREND-CONTINUATION and BREAKOUT. Volume clusters with empty space between = directional move through the gap. — 2.12.2 Volume Step as Support/Resistance — Definition: A volume step is a price level on the volume profile that acts as a barrier or support. — How it works: — 
> This is also the volume step here — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [END] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — 
> If we take out the 620 again, now this step up here, everything's cool. As long as we're below that, it could be that it tests the 600 again. This is the step here. — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [END] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — Execution mode context: TREND-CONTINUATION. Volume step = support/resistance level for position management. — 2.12.3 Bell of Trading Volume = Stop Reference — Definition: The area with the highest trading volume (the bell) is used as a reference for stop-loss placement. — How it works: — 
> This was where the bell of trading volume was, which is why the stop loss was set above the bell. — Source GPS: n7ZSkjb7JXs | [END] | $3,400 pre-market trade — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Bell of volume = logical stop reference. — 2.13 Time-of-Day / Session Proof — 2.13.1 Lunch Break = Low Volume — Definition: During lunch hours, market volume typically drops, making breakouts less reliable and reversals more likely. — How it works: — 
> if you take a look at the time here, you'll see it's midday. And during lunchtime, there's usually relatively little volume in the market. — Source GPS: wPMiyJXL0zU | [MIDDLE] | Champion breaks down trades — 
> the lunch break is coming up, so I'm ending the session. — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [END] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Low volume at lunch = counter-trade context (exhaustion more likely). — 2.13.2 Pre-Market = Different Numbers — Definition: Pre-market delta and volume numbers are not comparable to regular trading hours. — How it works: — 
> I'd like to quickly show you this pre-market. We are currently in a pre-market period. That means I have to base everything on different numbers. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE-END] | CVD Strategy — Execution mode context: Universal. Pre-market proof thresholds are different. — 2.13.3 Cash Open = High Risk — Definition: The period around the cash market open carries high risk of sharp moves. — How it works: — 
> the closer we get to the cash open where by the way I also want to reduce risk — Source GPS: n7ZSkjb7JXs | [END] | $3,400 pre-market trade — 
> The risk at the open is of course high that the market will make a quick sharp move and then you'll be really annoyed if you end up running the entire position into the red. — Source GPS: n7ZSkjb7JXs | [END] | $3,400 pre-market trade — Execution mode context: Universal. Cash open = reduce risk, tighten stops, or exit. — 2.14 Sentiment Proof — 2.14.1 UX Sentiment Check — Definition: Tom checks sentiment data (UX sentiment) to see if market participants are predominantly long or short. — How it works: — 
> I always check the UX sentiment. And what does that show me? It shows me that currently a huge number of people are long and yes, the small derivatives traders. — Source GPS: n7ZSkjb7JXs | [MIDDLE] | $3,400 pre-market trade — Execution mode context: COUNTER-TRADE. Extreme sentiment (everyone long at highs) = contrarian signal. — Gap: UX sentiment is a futures-specific tool. Crypto equivalent may be funding rates or long/short ratio, but Tom does not address crypto-specific sentiment tools. — END SECTION 2 — Section 3: Proof Combinations & Hierarchies — Tom rarely uses a single proof type in isolation. He layers multiple proofs. This section catalogs the combinations Tom explicitly demonstrates or describes. — 3.1 Counter-Trade: The Perfect Trade Combination — Tom's ideal counter-trade requires multiple parameters: — 1. Zone: Price at a predefined resistance/support area (not in the middle) — 2. Liquidity proof: No liquidity at the extreme, liquidity exhausted — 3. Structural proof: B profile, lower highs, no base, balance-heavy profiles — 4. Momentum proof: Momentum gone at tops, volumes rising but no activity at extreme — 5. Time context: Lunch break, pre-market, post-event — 6. Correlation: Multi-asset confirmation — Tom's own words on the perfect trade: — 
> I've now linked four things together that give me a good feeling. And that's how the perfect trade can come about for me, because I feel good about it. Because I have logic, because I have depth. — Source GPS: wPMiyJXL0zU | [MIDDLE] | Champion breaks down trades — 
> the most important thing of all, the whole thing took place in a price range where large, aggressive traders had already been active before. And for me that's the absolute priority. It has to have that, otherwise I wouldn't take such a trade, because if I just do it somewhere in the middle, that's not a good thing. — Source GPS: wPMiyJXL0zU | [MIDDLE] | Champion breaks down trades — Minimum combination for counter-trade: — Zone (mandatory) + No liquidity at extreme (mandatory) + At least one structural proof (B profile, lower highs, no base, finished auction) — 3.2 Trend-Continuation: Acceptance + Power + Direction — Tom's trend-continuation proof combination: — 1. Zone: Pullback to a key level (VWAP, value area, business zone) — 2. Acceptance proof: Price accepts the zone (closes above/below, holds) — 3. Volume proof: Volume confirms at the zone (aggressive buyers/sellers) — 4. Order flow proof: Unfinished auction, absorption, delta positive — 5. Speed proof: Market speed picking up after acceptance — 6. Structural proof: VPOC trending, VWAP holding — Example from live trade: — 
> The market opened lower, then crashed down, came back in, formed acceptance, then made a nice pull back to the business zone. And here it confirmed it again and showed us that there are aggressive buyers. — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [MIDDLE] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — 3.3 Breakout: Volume + Speed + Acceptance — Tom's breakout proof combination: — 1. Zone: Narrow range, consolidation, balance area — 2. Volume proof: Volume increasing, no extreme buyers/sellers in range — 3. Speed proof: Market speed picking up — 4. Acceptance proof: Close above/below the range — 5. CVD proof: Delta supports the breakout direction — Example: — 
> at the moment when I see right here that there is no longer strong support down here, I can see that here on the cumulative delta, it's not being strongly supported, so not being strongly bought. For me, it's very clear that the probability of the range breaking to the downside is high now. — Source GPS: y67l86cSqb8 | [MIDDLE] | CVD Strategy — 
> The speed is picking up. Look at what's being bought here. And now comes the breakout. — Source GPS: 4MoNjEQ2Dd8 | [END] | $2,350 in 40 Minutes — 3.4 Scalp: Sideways VWAP + No Breakout Assumption — Tom's scalp proof combination: — 1. Zone: Price near VWAP in a sideways market — 2. No breakout proof: No volume, no speed, no acceptance — 3. Quick exit: Small target, quick in and out — Source GPS: setup_type_rules.md | SC-1.1 | From BVpYGKz9PBU — 3.5 Proof Hierarchy — Not all proofs are equal. Tom's hierarchy (inferred from live trades): — Tier 1 (mandatory): Zone identification. Without a zone, there is no trade. — Tier 2 (primary proof): Liquidity status (no liquidity at extreme, volume in, absorption). — Tier 3 (secondary proof): Order flow (finished/unfinished auction, delta, footprint). — Tier 4 (confirming proof): Structural (B profile, lower highs, VPOC trend). — Tier 5 (context): Correlation, time-of-day, sentiment. — A trade requires at least Tier 1 + one Tier 2 proof. Additional tiers increase confidence but are not mandatory. — END SECTION 3 — Section 4: Proof → Execution Mode Matrix — This matrix maps which proof types are most relevant to which execution modes. — | Proof Type | CT | TC | BO | SC | Notes | |
